WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.750
When is a focus group the right research choice?

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.750
When hearing people respond to one another can help explain a shared

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.000
experience or idea.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:16.857
A moderator asks open questions and explores the language, differences and assumptions

00:00:16.857 --> 00:00:18.000
that emerge.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
The interaction is part of the evidence.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.222
A focus group does not tell you what percentage of an entire

00:00:28.222 --> 00:00:29.259
population agrees.

00:00:29.259 --> 00:00:35.481
And a private or sensitive experience may be better discussed one to

00:00:35.481 --> 00:00:36.000
one.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.417
Before commissioning, clarify who will recruit, moderate, analyze and report.

00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:47.917
Agree the audience, research question, consent arrangements and what the findings need

00:00:47.917 --> 00:00:49.000
to inform.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.552
Think of the group as a conversation lens, not a vote.

00:00:53.552 --> 00:00:58.517
Choose it because the discussion can illuminate the decision, not because a

00:00:58.517 --> 00:01:01.000
fixed number of sessions sounds convenient.

